--- reading it --------------------------------------------------------------------------
Result: PASS, the previous version (1c7e147), booted slot, root read-only, data intact.

How early the cut was: the pre-install handler takes about a second (16:32:52 to :53 in the
FR-009 capture), and nothing follows its start line, so the plug came out inside that second.
That is before "Marking target slot ... as non-bootable (bad)", so nothing had been written
and BOOT_ORDER never changed: this trial shows that an install interrupted before its first
write leaves the device exactly as it was. It is the "early" point of the procedure, and by
construction the least dangerous one. The windows that could hurt (after the slot is marked
bad, during the write, in the flush at 99%) are trials 2 to 5.

Seen and expected: /data replayed its journal ("recovery complete"), which is the unclean
shutdown; U-Boot read the redundant copy of its environment, which is only the other of the
two alternating copies being the newer one. Both boots after the cut behaved normally.
